Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and how they will be used  

• Person.PID : primary key of every actor – needed to recognise Shahrukh Khan, his co‑actors and the second–degree actors.  
• Person.Name : to locate the row that contains ‘Shahrukh Khan’.  
• M_Cast.PID : actor who belongs to the cast of a film – joins Person ↔ M_Cast.  
• M_Cast.MID : film in which that actor appeared – joins M_Cast ↔ Movie and lets us move from one actor to another through common movies.  
• Movie.MID : unique id of a film – used when we want the film’s title or just to keep the list of SRK’s movies.  
• Movie.title : optional, only to display human‑readable titles of the movies we fetch.  

Conditions that will be useful  

1. Person.Name LIKE '%Shahrukh%'  → find Shahrukh Khan’s PID.  
2. "PID" <> (that PID)             → exclude SRK himself where necessary.  
3. "MID"  IN (SELECT …)            → keep only movies belonging to a set (e.g. SRK’s movies).  
4. "PID" NOT IN (SELECT …)         → remove the direct co‑actors when we finally count the actors that have Shahrukh‑number = 2.  

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Below are 10 exploratory → final SQL queries (each limited to at most 20 rows except the final COUNT) showing the whole reasoning chain.

```sql
-- 1) Shahrukh Khan’s record in the Person table
SELECT * 
FROM "Person" 
WHERE "Name" LIKE '%Shahrukh%' 
